        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          Expedition One carried the first long-duration occupants of the International Space Station (ISS): Expedition Commander William Shepherd, Soyuz Commander Yuri Gidzenko, and Russion Flight Engineer Sergei Krikalev. The mission launched on October 31, 2000, in a Soyuz rocket from Baikonur Cosmodrome in Russia.
<P>
The main objective of the Expedition One mission was to prepare the space station for future scientific research. During the four-month mission, the ISS crew hosted three Shuttle crews. The Shuttles brought the U.S. Laboratory Module Destiny, the first science racks, photovoltaic arrays to augment ISS power capability, and a variety of other key hardware.
<P>
Shuttle crews conducted flights to the ISS over the two years prior to Expedition One delivering logistical supplies. The Expedition One crew activated critical life support systems and unpacked station components, clothing, laptop computers, office equipment, cables and electrical gear left behind by the previous Shuttle crews. In addition to activating these systems and unpacking, the Expedition One crew unloaded three unmanned Russian Progress resupply vehicles. By "moving in" to their new home, the Expedition One crew set the stage for a continuous human presence in space by international researchers for at least the next 15 years.
<P>
Although science activities were somewhat modest for the Expedition One crew, experiments were conducted that focused mainly on material and life sciences. Among these experiments were Protein Crystal Growth, Seed Growth Kit, EarthKAM, Crew Earth Observations, Middeck Active Control Experiment, Spatial Differences in CO2 Concentration, Interim Resistive Exercise Device, and three Treadmill Vibration Isolation System experiments. Several other Russian experiments were flown to the ISS aboard visiting Progress vehicles involving research in the fields of biotechnology, biomedical research, geophysical research, and engineering.
<P>
The Expedition One crewmembers returned to Earth March 21, 2001, aboard the Space Shuttle Discovery at the completion of the STS-102 mission, which brought the second resident crew to the ISS to begin scientific research in earnest.
        </div>

